    Fixes #1563: inferior call stale cache (#3813)
    
    * Add regression test for stale cache after inferior function calls (#1563)
    
    Test that `thread_arena` remains accessible after many `malloc()` calls
    made via `gdb.execute('call (void *)malloc(...)')` from Python.
    
    Without a fix, GDB's `call` command does not fire `gdb.events.stop`, so
    caches (most importantly vmmap) become stale when the heap outgrows the
    cached memory map via `brk`, causing "Cannot build heap object on an
    unmapped address".
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.6 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * Clear stop-caches after inferior function calls via `gdb.events.inferior_call`
    
    GDB does not fire `gdb.events.stop` for inferior function calls (e.g.
    `call malloc(0x20)` from the prompt or `gdb.execute('call ...')` from
    Python).  This means caches keyed on "stop" — most importantly vmmap —
    become stale when the inferior's memory layout changes during such calls
    (e.g. `brk` expanding the heap).
    
    Hook `gdb.events.inferior_call` (available since GDB 13) to call
    `clear_cache(CacheUntilEvent.STOP)` after each `InferiorCallPostEvent`,
    so that subsequent reads see up-to-date state.
    
    Fixes #1563
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.6 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
